we just flew back from pax and pour our  arms tired nope nope nope not making  that dumb joke but our hands are a bit  tired from playing a bunch of great  games you might not have heard about so  here are seven of our favorites that you  might want to put on your radar first up  black future 88 though maybe they should  call it black Future 18 because you only  have 18 minutes to get to the top of a  procedurally generated tower and kill  the bad guy before your heart explodes  but you'll probably be dead before that  happens anyway so yeah the game is all  about taking multiple runs up the tower  switching weapons every chance you get  and the fact that it has a great art  style and synth soundtrack is just icing  on the indie cake city of brass is  another game where you'll be making  multiple runs but this time it's to the  center of a fabled Arabian city full of  treasure you've got a sword you've got a  whip and you've got a bunch of enemies  and traps to get past if you're hoping  to reach the end the randomly generated  stages only get tougher the closer to  the middle you get and if the look or  gameplay kind of reminds you of Bioshock  that's because it's made by a team of  developers who worked on that series -  speaking of games that remind you of  other games the messenger does for Ninja  Gaiden what shovel Knights did for  Megaman and that's kind of all you need  right there I mean come on who doesn't  love this little guy anyway the  messenger starts as a deceptively simple  8-bit platformer but later on turns into  a much more elaborate 16-bit game and if  you think that's the last twist it has  to offer you'd be wrong expect to see a  bunch of clever uses of the time and art  swap during your adventure that's not  the only side scroller with a great look  either dark devotion puts you in the  shoes of a Templar exploring a  mysterious temple but here's the thing  every time you open a door and choose a  path inside the temple the door closes  behind you there's no going back but at  least you can loot weapons and relics  along the way faith is also a tangible  substance for your Templar who can use  it to open chests and unlock secret  paths just be careful not to lose your  faith both figuratively and literally  let's relax a bit now the stillness of  the wind is definitely the calmest game  on this list and it's all about taking  care of goats the elderly tall  is the last person living in her village  and you can guide her through her daily  routine of raising goats making cheese  growing vegetables cooking meals and  trading with the merchants and yet  there's still an air of unease here  since this way of life probably can't  continue for much longer  you can also set out with donut county  this puzzle game definitely reminds me  of Katamari where you collect objects to  grow bigger and bigger but instead of  rolling a ball you maneuver a little  hole in the ground the more stuff you  swallow the bigger it grows and you can  even solve puzzles using the items  you've got up swallow in Adobe oven for  example and you'll be able to use the  heat to send a hot air balloon into the  sky let's finish up with one more game  that's also about dropping objects into  holes if you're tired of my segues don't  worry this is the last one pool panic is  much more than a simple game of  billiards each level introduces some  crazy new concepts like bear balls that  growl at you whenever you try to cue up  but your goal is still to knock each  ball into the pocket of course you might  find some hidden balls and even more  bonus stages along the way too and  you've gotta love the look of it you can  just tell this as an Adult Swim joint so  there you have it  7 of our favorite games from PAX East  2018 if you saw something you loved that  we didn't talk about here tell us all  about it in the comments and if you have  your own thoughts on our picks let us  know  you
 

